Z||000000||IAIA||03-01-2001||03:17 PM||||I'm gonna get a job soon and save up and by the end of the summer hopefully I will have enough to get my first car. I originally wanted an old 69 Charger but it would cost to much money to restore so I thought it best to get a newer car. I rather have an American car then some Rice Mobile so I'm thinking about getting a Fiero. I want an '88 Fiero GT. How hard is it to find these? I really want the Fastbacked GT because I don't really like Notchbacks that much. Any info you got would be cool.<P>------------------<BR><B><I><BR>IAIA: The International Automotive Image Archive<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/crxzero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/crxzero</A> <P></I></B>||||reg||10
Z||000001||Wade87GT||03-01-2001||05:29 PM||||Welcome IAIA,<P>The 88 GT and Formulas are the best handling of all the stock Fieros. If you really want an 88 make sure you get a good one, since some parts are not as plentiful as the other years. Search <A HREF="http://www.hemmings.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hemmings.com/</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.traderonline.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.traderonline.com/</A>  for starters. Don't forget to use Pennock's search engine for tech info and The Mall.<p>[This message has been edited by Wade87GT (edited 03-01-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||87GTZ34||03-01-2001||05:50 PM||||There is an '88 GT with only 30K mi and the guy only wants $6300 for it.  Here's the number 727-372-7644.  It is in the auto trader online if you want to see a pic.  When I selected the area I selected within 200 mi of WPB so its not too far (at least not like CA or something)||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GT||03-01-2001||07:31 PM||||The Car 87GTZ34 is talking about is located 30 miles to the northwest of Tampa, FL.  Its a really nice car, its been stored 7 years.  He said that it still had the original tires on it, the A/C needs to be fixed, and he didnt service the tranny or cooling system.  He said he drives it about once a week to work.  It is white with tan interior, and a automatic tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000004||IAIA||03-01-2001||11:58 PM||||Wow thanks for the info guys. I'm not in the market just yet, but I will be by the end of this summer. Whats this I hear about some of the Fieros catching on fire? Is that just the '84s? Or is there a chance it could happen to any of them?<P>Also what is a Formula? Is that a Fastback? is that the V6 Fiero I've heard about?<P>------------------<BR><B><I><BR>IAIA: The International Automotive Image Archive<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/crxzero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/crxzero</A> <P></I></B><p>[This message has been edited by IAIA (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||RossT||03-02-2001||01:27 AM||||check out ogre's site about the "F" word. <A HREF="http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/tunnel6/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://theogre.home.sprynet.com/tunnel6/index.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Voytek||03-02-2001||06:22 PM||||Some of the early 4 cyl fieros had an engine problem that was later recalled and fixed.  The fire hazard with a Fiero is no more than with any other car.  Fieros are some of the easier cars to work on with endless possibilities (engine, body, etc).  I would get an '87 fastback, though.  There is more parts available.  The suspension was beefed up in '87 and the 5 spd became available on the GT.<BR>The formula was only available in '88 and is not the fastback style.  The fastback is probably the nicest style, though.  Also, the 87's tend to be slightly less expensive.<BR>Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000007||IAIA||03-03-2001||12:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Some of the early 4 cyl fieros had an engine problem that was later recalled and fixed.  The fire hazard with a Fiero is no more than with any other car.  Fieros are some of the easier cars to work on with endless possibilities (engine, body, etc).  I would get an '87 fastback, though.  There is more parts available.  The suspension was beefed up in '87 and the 5 spd became available on the GT.<BR>The formula was only available in '88 and is not the fastback style.  The fastback is probably the nicest style, though.  Also, the 87's tend to be slightly less expensive.<BR>Good Luck!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought the GT's were Fastbacks?<P>------------------<BR><B><I><BR>IAIA: The International Automotive Image Archive<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/crxzero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/crxzero</A> <P></I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||03-03-2001||12:18 AM||||dig around more before buying one. Each year has it's own quirks. 88 may have a better suspension but like all other years has it's own problems.<P>Many of 88's suspension improvments can be had on other years thru third party parts. (Just replacing the OE cradel bushings with Pollyurethane or aluminum makes a huge improvement.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||AusFiero||03-03-2001||07:06 AM||||Interesting thought Ogre....Would that explain why my 86 1/2 GT feels like the rear end kicks to the left when I give it a bootful ? ...The bushes are new GM replaced 5000kms ago.||||reg||1
