Z||000012||Old Lar||03-03-2001||11:58 AM||||IAIA: You're gonna get a job soon!  A $2000 Fiero will more than likely need $1000 in fixes, then there is the need for insurance, and gas money. The newest Fiero is 13 years old and will need lots of maintenance. Mommy and daddy are going to fork over lots of money for their kid to have a toy car or your job, which you'll get soon, pays lots of money.<P>88 GT's are more expensive to fix just suspension components, if you can locate the parts.  Tim@fieroparts.com has a loaded 88GT with 117 miles on it for sale for $20K or so.||||reg||3
Z||000013||IAIA||03-03-2001||11:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B><BR>88 GT's are more expensive to fix just suspension components, if you can locate the parts.  Tim@fieroparts.com has a loaded 88GT with 117 miles on it for sale for $20K or so.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hahahahhahahahahhahahahahahahhahahaha 20k. I could get a Camaro for that much. Or the car I wanted in the first place.<P>If I got a Fiero I'm not gonna make it into some SuperStreet Racing Fiero, so why would I have to buy 18k worth of crap for it. I want it to get from Point A-B. I doesn't have to look or run like brand new, just get the job done.||||reg||1
